---
/**
 * What GRPO actually optimises: not the reward, but the reward minus the group's own mean.
 *
 * Two groups of eight candidates. The left one has spread -- the rewards come from
 * `rukh.train.rewards` over eight plausible moves in one position -- and its baseline sits where
 * the mean is, so four candidates get pushed up and four get pushed down. The right one is flat:
 * every sample is the same move, every reward is the same number, the baseline lands on top of
 * all of them and every advantage is zero. The engine was paid for both.
 *
 * That second group is not a hypothetical. It is the reason `GrpoResult` carries `flat_groups`
 * separately from `groups`: the count says how much of the budget the model's own confidence is
 * throwing away, and there is no way to know it in advance.
 *
 * The dashed line settles onto the group's own mean on a loop (`fig-group-settle`, in
 * `global.css`), because the subtraction is the one step readers reconstruct wrongly: they expect
 * the tallest bar to win, and what wins is the bar furthest from the line. What the optimiser
 * consumes is the arrow from the line to the top of each bar, so those arrows are the thing that
 * appears once the line has settled: the bars themselves are neutral and the sign lives in the
 * arrows. Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the line is parked on the mean and the arrows are simply
 * shown. The arrows' keyframes (`fig-group-arrow`) live in `global.css` next to
 * `fig-group-settle`, so they ship with the page regardless of how the component's own styles are
 * hoisted.
 */
const left = [0.98, 0.74, 1.21, 0.31, 0.62, 0.05, 0.88, 0.45];
const right = [0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74];

const mean = (values: number[]) => values.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / values.length;

const bar = (value: number) => Math.max(2, value * 74);
const columns = (values: number[], originX: number) =>
  values.map((value, index) => ({ value, x: originX + index * 26 }));

const panels = [
  {
    title: 'un grupo con dispersión',
    values: left,
    originX: 46,
    verdict: 'ocho ventajas, cuatro arriba y cuatro abajo',
  },
  {
    title: 'un grupo que se repite',
    values: right,
    originX: 366,
    verdict: 'ocho ceros: el motor ya está pagado',
  },
];
---

<figure class="fig fig--group" tabindex="0">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 640 250"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Dos grupos de ocho candidatas. En el de la izquierda las recompensas van de 0,05 a 1,21 y la media cae en 0,655: desde esa línea sale una flecha hasta cada barra, cuatro hacia arriba y cuatro hacia abajo, que son las ventajas que el optimizador consume. En el de la derecha las ocho valen 0,74, la media cae encima de todas y no hay flecha que dibujar: las ocho ventajas son cero, aunque el motor se haya pagado igual."
  >
    <defs>
      <marker
        id="fig-group-up"
        viewBox="0 0 8 8"
        refX="4"
        refY="7"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M 0 8 L 4 0 L 8 8 z" fill="var(--accent)"></path>
      </marker>
      <marker
        id="fig-group-down"
        viewBox="0 0 8 8"
        refX="4"
        refY="7"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M 0 8 L 4 0 L 8 8 z" fill="var(--ink)"></path>
      </marker>
    </defs>
    <g font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--muted)">
      {panels.map((panel) => {
        const baseline = mean(panel.values);
        const baselineY = 160 - bar(baseline);
        return (
          <g>
            <text x={panel.originX - 36} y="24" fill="var(--ink)">
              {panel.title}
            </text>
            {columns(panel.values, panel.originX).map((column) => (
              <rect
                x={column.x}
                y={160 - bar(column.value)}
                width="16"
                height={bar(column.value)}
                rx="1.5"
                fill="var(--diagram-stroke)"
                fill-opacity="0.2"
                stroke="var(--line)"
                stroke-width="0.8"
              />
            ))}
            <g class="fig-group__baseline">
              <line
                x1={panel.originX - 8}
                y1={baselineY}
                x2={panel.originX + 8 * 26}
                y2={baselineY}
                stroke="var(--accent)"
                stroke-width="1.2"
                stroke-dasharray="4 3"
              ></line>
              <text
                x={panel.originX + 8 * 26 + 4}
                y={baselineY + 3}
                font-size="9"
                fill="var(--accent-text-aa)"
              >
                {baseline.toFixed(2)}
              </text>
            </g>
            <g class="fig-group__arrow">
              {columns(panel.values, panel.originX).map((column) => {
                const top = 160 - bar(column.value);
                const up = column.value > baseline + 0.005;
                const flat = Math.abs(column.value - baseline) <= 0.005;
                const cx = column.x + 8;
                if (flat) {
                  return (
                    <circle cx={cx} cy={baselineY} r="2.2" fill="var(--ink)" fill-opacity="0.7" />
                  );
                }
                return (
                  <line
                    x1={cx}
                    y1={baselineY}
                    x2={cx}
                    y2={up ? top + 5 : top - 5}
                    stroke={up ? 'var(--accent)' : 'var(--ink)'}
                    stroke-width="1.4"
                    marker-end={up ? 'url(#fig-group-up)' : 'url(#fig-group-down)'}
                  />
                );
              })}
            </g>
            <line
              x1={panel.originX - 8}
              y1="160"
              x2={panel.originX + 8 * 26}
              y2="160"
              stroke="var(--line)"
              stroke-width="0.8"
            ></line>
            <text x={panel.originX - 36} y="182" font-size="9">
              recompensa
            </text>
            <text x={panel.originX - 36} y="206" font-size="9" fill="var(--accent-text-aa)">
              {panel.verdict}
            </text>
          </g>
        );
      })}
      <text x="46" y="232" font-size="9">
        la línea a trazos es la media del propio grupo; las flechas desde ella hasta cada barra
      </text>
      <text x="46" y="244" font-size="9">
        son las ventajas: eso, y no la altura, es lo que se empuja
      </text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="caption">
    GRPO no optimiza la recompensa: optimiza la recompensa menos la media de su propio grupo. Es la
    nota sobre la media de la clase: un 7 no dice nada hasta que sabes que la media fue un 5, y si
    toda la clase saca un 7 nadie destaca. Un grupo que se repite entero tiene esa media encima de
    todo y no aporta un solo gradiente.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
